// Lesson 3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy
// Problem breakdown: spawning, aiming, moving, and cleaning up projectiles.
// Comparing three ways to move a projectile, and two ways to aim it.
// GROUND TRUTH: Projectile.cs is an empty stub but IS already attached to
// Turret_Projectile.prefab. That prefab has NO Rigidbody2D and NO Collider2D,
// so any physics-based movement option costs the student an added component.
// Turret.prefab has a child "SpawnPoint" that serves as the muzzle.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret knows when you are near; now it needs teeth. Spawning and firing a projectile
    looks complicated, but it breaks into four smaller problems:
    <strong>spawn a clone → aim it → move it → clean it up</strong>. Each of those has more than
    one valid solution, and today you pick yours on purpose.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Firing a projectile means coordinating four separate engine features:</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Sub-problem</th><th>Unity API</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Spawning</strong></td>
            <td><code>Instantiate(prefab, pos, rot)</code></td>
            <td>Creates a brand-new clone of a prefab at runtime and drops it into the scene hierarchy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Aiming</strong></td>
            <td><code>(target - origin).normalized</code></td>
            <td>Vector subtraction gives a vector pointing at the target; <code>.normalized</code> shrinks its length to exactly 1 so it describes <em>direction only</em>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Moving</strong></td>
            <td><code>Transform</code> or <code>Rigidbody2D</code></td>
            <td>Advances the projectile every frame. Three different ways — see the table below.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>4. Cleanup</strong></td>
            <td><code>Destroy(gameObject, delay)</code></td>
            <td>Destroys the clone after a time delay, so bullets that miss do not fly forever.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Choosing How Your Bullet Flies</h3>
        <p>Three legitimate ways to move a projectile. Read the cost column carefully — two of these require a component the prefab does not have:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it requires</th><th>Pick it when</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Translate by a direction vector</strong></td>
                <td>Keep the aim direction from spawn and offset the position by <code>direction * speed * Time.deltaTime</code> each frame.</td>
                <td>Nothing. Works with the prefab as it ships.</td>
                <td>Straight-flying shots that pass over or through everything.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Rotate, then move along local forward</strong></td>
                <td>Point the sprite at the target once, then translate along its own right axis each frame.</td>
                <td>Nothing extra, but more maths.</td>
                <td>When the sprite should visibly <em>face</em> the way it travels — arrows, spears, missiles.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Rigidbody2D velocity</strong></td>
                <td>Set the body's velocity once and let the physics engine carry it.</td>
                <td><strong>A Rigidbody2D</strong>, which the projectile does not have yet.</td>
                <td>Bullets that should bounce off walls, or be stopped by them.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>Open <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code> and read its Inspector. Which of the three approaches can you build <em>right now</em>, and which one costs you an added component?</li>
            <li>If you want bullets that ricochet off cave walls like a pinball, which approach is dramatically easier, and what would the other two require you to build yourself?</li>
            <li>For a beam or a magic missile that flies straight through scenery, why is the direction-vector approach both simpler <em>and</em> more correct?</li>
            <li>Why is <code>Destroy(gameObject, delay)</code> essential? What happens to your frame rate if a player dodges a thousand bullets and nothing ever removes them?</li>
            <li>Your turret's range check runs every frame. If you fire from inside that check, how many bullets per second are you creating — and is that a design decision or an accident?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Each snippet in isolation, from a system that is not a video game. Work out what maps onto
        your projectile.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — Computing a heading on an aircraft navigation system:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// (destination - current) gives direction; .normalized scales the length to 1.0
Vector3 heading = (waypoint.position - transform.position).normalized;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — Converting a direction to an angle on a weather vane:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Atan2 turns X/Y components into an angle in radians; Rad2Deg converts to degrees
float windAngle = Mathf.Atan2(wind.y, wind.x) * Mathf.Rad2Deg;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Building a 2D rotation from that angle:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// In 2D, rotation happens around the Z axis - the one pointing out of the screen
transform.rotation = Quaternion.AngleAxis(windAngle, Vector3.forward);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — Spawning and initialising on a fountain:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Stamp a clone, then reach into it to hand over the data it needs
GameObject orb = Instantiate(orbPrefab, nozzle.position, Quaternion.identity);
orb.GetComponent&lt;MagicOrb&gt;().InitializeOrb(targetCoords);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — Timed self-cleanup on a soap bubble:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Schedules this object to be destroyed automatically 4 seconds from now
Destroy(gameObject, 4.0f);</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In Example 1, what happens if you leave off <code>.normalized</code>? If the waypoint is 20 units away and you multiply that vector by a speed, what goes wrong?</li>
            <li>Why is <code>Vector3.forward</code> — that is, (0, 0, 1) — the axis of rotation for a 2D sprite?</li>
            <li>In Example 4, why does the fountain reach <em>into the clone</em> to pass data, rather than the clone finding the target on its own? Which approach would you pick for a bullet, and why?</li>
            <li>Example 4 uses <code>Quaternion.identity</code> for the spawn rotation. Is that the right choice for a bullet that is about to be aimed? What does <code>identity</code> actually mean?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Open <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code>. You will find an empty
        <code>Projectile</code> component <em>already attached</em> — same pattern as the turret.
        Your job is to fill it in.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for <code>Projectile.cs</code>:</strong>

        <p><strong>Variables and tuning knobs:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What serialized float knobs should a designer be able to turn? You need at least a speed and a lifetime — are there others your chosen approach implies?</li>
            <li>What defaults make sense? Think about how big the Overworld is and how far a bullet should reasonably travel before giving up.</li>
            <li>Your projectile needs to remember its direction (or its target). What type holds that, and where does it get set?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Being initialised at spawn:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>When the turret creates a bullet, it needs to tell that bullet where to go. What <em>public</em> method signature receives that information? Why must it be public?</li>
            <li>How do you compute a direction from the spawn point to the target? Why normalize it?</li>
            <li>If you chose the rotate-then-move approach: which trigonometric function gives you an angle from X and Y, and how do you convert radians to degrees?</li>
            <li>If you chose the rotate-then-move approach: which axis does a 2D sprite rotate around, and which method builds a rotation from an angle and an axis?</li>
            <li>How do you schedule the bullet's own destruction so it cleans up even if it never hits anything?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Flight, every frame:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which method moves the transform each frame, and how do you keep the speed frame-rate independent?</li>
            <li>If you rotated the sprite, which local direction is "forward" for a 2D sprite? If you did not rotate it, what do you translate by instead?</li>
            <li>Why does the direction get computed once at spawn rather than recomputed every frame? What weapon would re-aiming every frame actually give you?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for <code>TurretController.cs</code>:</strong>

        <p><strong>Inspector slots:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What serialized fields let a designer hand the turret a projectile to fire and a place to fire it from? (Remember the child object you noted in <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>.)</li>
            <li>What type holds a reference to a prefab — the asset, not an instance?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The firing method:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What guard check stops the method when a designer has left a slot empty?</li>
            <li>Which method stamps a clone into the scene? What position and rotation should it start with?</li>
            <li>How do you reach the <code>Projectile</code> component on the clone you just made, and what do you call on it?</li>
            <li>Which object's position do you pass as the target — and should you pass a <em>position</em>, or the player's Transform?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Triggering it:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Where inside your existing range check does the firing method get called?</li>
            <li>Which slots must be wired in the Inspector before you test?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save, wire the slots, and press Play. Walk into range: a projectile appears at the muzzle
        and travels. Do not celebrate the fire rate yet — you are currently firing
        <em>every single frame</em>, because your range check runs every frame. That is exactly
        the problem the next lesson solves.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Projectile Faults</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Leave the projectile prefab slot unassigned and step into range. What error appears, and which line of your code is it pointing at?</li>
            <li>Comment out the <code>Destroy</code> line, fire for ten seconds, then look at the Hierarchy in play mode. How many objects are there? What will that do to the game over a five-minute session?</li>
            <li>Remove <code>.normalized</code> from your direction calculation. What happens when the player is far away versus close? Explain the relationship between distance and speed that you just created.</li>
            <li>Pass the player's <em>Transform</em> into your spawn method instead of their position, and have the bullet read <code>targetTransform.position</code> every frame. What kind of weapon did you accidentally build?</li>
            <li>If you chose the rotate-then-move approach, translate by <code>Vector3.up</code> instead of the forward direction. Which way do bullets now travel relative to where the sprite points?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Bullet tuning</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Tune your speed knob from something slow and dodgeable to something that feels like a sniper. At what point does the turret stop being a challenge and start being unfair? What else could you change to keep it fair at high speed?</li>
            <li>Change the lifetime knob so bullets die almost immediately, then so they live far too long. What is the right lifetime for a bullet that should threaten the player but not wander the whole map?</li>
            <li>Your bullets currently fly through everything — walls included. Is that a problem for this game? What would you have to add to a bullet to make a wall stop it, and which of the three movement approaches from the docs would then be the better choice?</li>
            <li>Fire one bullet and watch it in the Hierarchy. What is the clone named, and where does it sit in the hierarchy? Does that tell you anything about <code>Instantiate</code> you did not expect?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Break projectile firing into spawning, aiming, moving and cleanup</li>
            <li>Explain the role of <code>Instantiate</code> in stamping runtime clones</li>
            <li>Explain why a direction vector is normalized, and what goes wrong without it</li>
            <li>Compare the three ways to move a projectile, and state what each one costs</li>
            <li>Explain why your turret currently fires every frame</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — the projectile lifecycle and aiming in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Instantiate, Destroy and Mathf</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="3.3.html">3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</a>, where you fix the bullet hose you just built.</p>
</div>`;
